#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe539af-1253-5ce3-a65b-eb50ed639c52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curl of a Vector Field Using the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf8b6d97-79a7-54e4-9d97-eaee97362d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,4,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369016} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa0dddf-66e0-5312-ba7d-0527083a5dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369017} {:answer/id #uuid "21641dfe-2aa8-592c-9a2a-0dc6991ffabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369018} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac61db9-581c-5407-8871-55a536d13738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369019} {:answer/id #uuid "2f605ade-6095-5f65-9446-8ebbfe1caefe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,-2,3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21641dfe-2aa8-592c-9a2a-0dc6991ffabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,-1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369018} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce8719f-c082-5922-bad1-e0173cb1e489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8abe39b-bb92-55f7-bb84-89cdb536b574" :question/math-academy-id "q-105895" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-1,0,x{y}^{3}⟩$ and the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=yz.$ Given that\n\n$$\n∇\\times F=⟨3x{y}^{2},-{y}^{3},0⟩,\\,∇f=⟨0,z,y⟩,\n$$\n\ncalculate $∇\\times (f\\,F)$ at the point $(0,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ -1 & 0 & x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[z(x{y}^{3})-y(0)]i-[0(x{y}^{3})-y(-1)]j+[0(0)-z(-1)]k \\\\  & =xz{y}^{3}\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =yz⟨3x{y}^{2},-{y}^{3},0⟩+⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3xz{y}^{3},-z{y}^{4},0⟩+⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4xz{y}^{3},-z{y}^{4}-y,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(0,-1,2) & =⟨4(0)(2)(-1)^{3},-2(-1)^{4}-(-1),2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85388f7d-b2ee-520b-b711-a7721c6f620c" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Variance of a Transformed Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d3a88c-7ad8-57fd-9c72-7b52d059228f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369023} {:answer/id #uuid "a3bb1365-7088-5b65-86dd-73e3bcd87292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369024} {:answer/id #uuid "10e4f492-372c-569e-ab2a-68c9e0bb25d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369025} {:answer/id #uuid "fa84e3a5-6f80-5b1a-8f2b-de06bf6d89be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369026} {:answer/id #uuid "0cad8773-dfb7-510f-b62a-c1f0ba7a323a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10e4f492-372c-569e-ab2a-68c9e0bb25d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369025} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c7f961-8bd0-5161-8904-d148e0c79084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fac7001d-ff73-507a-96fa-7c0e89246538" :question/math-academy-id "q-109090" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4].$ \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=0.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot f(-1)+{0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18abaef5-2dc8-5961-bd2a-e6cc52cce7bb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Squared Random Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0b8edcc-96fd-5f4f-b5d1-f14e6777bf16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369030} {:answer/id #uuid "208d15e2-290f-5d19-90a7-cac6761f6e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369031} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b781f1-21d5-516f-a56f-2aba6d4471d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369032} {:answer/id #uuid "91fe8502-88a9-5946-bb20-f9f217bfbc2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369033} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbe2db0-dc18-5d7c-840a-19ecaec379f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfbe2db0-dc18-5d7c-840a-19ecaec379f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369034} :answer-field/id #uuid "01a580a8-b326-5f5d-842f-75c055c84bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "499118d7-5d2d-512d-85b0-2761f7186709" :question/math-academy-id "q-110220" :question/problem "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nCalculate $P({X}^{2}=9).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first add an ${X}^{2}$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $9$ | $81$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third rows of our table, we can immediately conclude that \n\n$$\nP({X}^{2}=9)=0.35+0.1=0.45.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e247e2c9-3e47-5cf4-be8d-f1f22bb30b39" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability Containing Inequalities From a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8718fa61-5101-5d42-8ff5-a4623b342288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369037} {:answer/id #uuid "5b01b0ad-f144-5698-8cca-3d3fac82494a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369038} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa2ed43-626c-52f6-bd69-46f64fcc1498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369039} {:answer/id #uuid "b76e551d-542c-5953-b371-3eb6aa5c1290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369040} {:answer/id #uuid "91dc9fea-f836-5738-a361-7ddb57dd111c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b01b0ad-f144-5698-8cca-3d3fac82494a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369038} :answer-field/id #uuid "db0d6d73-e8ba-5736-8685-c62c1ec3e267" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "698a78eb-ef71-5b2a-bf3e-b4f41a43c1f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-111083" :question/problem "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(0,1),(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(0,1))+P((X,Y)=(1,1)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,1)) \\\\  & =f(0,1)+f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(2,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.1+0.25 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Carrying Capacity of a Logistic Growth Model In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "956c66aa-326f-5a06-be61-c05003bfa3eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369044} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca48a55-b183-5ba6-bab7-3d758f101f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369045} {:answer/id #uuid "74a3ebf5-b294-5497-baf7-ae35b4e1f967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369046} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d8fc59-52e9-5ee5-9939-6579d13a3e20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369047} {:answer/id #uuid "8800b3d1-7cf8-51e0-944b-60d87f9f0afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "850" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74a3ebf5-b294-5497-baf7-ae35b4e1f967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369046} :answer-field/id #uuid "2458073f-13ac-5d6c-aef7-398cb69aef1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21e1d7d5-e8e8-5e32-9fd2-ba17c7038c72" :question/math-academy-id "q-118552" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of Weddell seals in a certain region of Antarctica follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.2y-0.002{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. According to this model, what is the maximum possible seal population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.2y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of seals cannot exceed $600.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82dbdc2f-390b-5464-afc5-a6e0401eb8e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369051} {:answer/id #uuid "ea11022f-b894-57fc-8592-605dd62043ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369052} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4c754b-eeac-5261-b97f-ae67f63753ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369053} {:answer/id #uuid "10c5d6ce-a270-5504-870e-d1547fa6d4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369054} {:answer/id #uuid "ce84bfcc-1740-5f0a-9e05-b303c55f346b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10c5d6ce-a270-5504-870e-d1547fa6d4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369054} :answer-field/id #uuid "15aa5794-c6e7-58c5-875f-8b6c68d5310f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4f38637-761c-59db-ada2-8df3d6501eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-126031" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(2<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x+1}]}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "037f2a02-949f-5dfc-893e-9e985cfb18b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369058} {:answer/id #uuid "4f7a71c8-2717-5dce-8fd2-f0246d31c46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369059} {:answer/id #uuid "85d04224-86f0-5523-9d5e-be8017437461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369060} {:answer/id #uuid "a1dfe140-fa66-5a20-8ca7-81a106a9005b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369061} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5289b5-b1e7-52cb-bb1d-a62a362a4647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85d04224-86f0-5523-9d5e-be8017437461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369060} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d74619b-474d-5689-a782-c6516775daf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6bc59286-96e6-53b4-9925-d6577fe6687e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127319" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨0,0,1⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨2,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =i \\\\  & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,0⟩| \\\\  & =|2+0+0| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93fb6c44-5d1c-548d-b12a-c2d42c762c26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 49\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294260} {:answer/id #uuid "7bee1c28-a017-513f-aec8-d20f4a003bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294261} {:answer/id #uuid "0388cfc5-c8d1-5fc5-9831-f028d3cb6239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-49 & -8 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294262} {:answer/id #uuid "50b87d23-088b-577f-87fa-71e62dd568a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}49 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294263} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff4399e-ed7b-55f3-a569-294cd45859c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 49\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50b87d23-088b-577f-87fa-71e62dd568a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}49 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294263} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c833db1-4dfe-5b56-8c61-d74f13993f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a1381b5-29b5-5930-b404-a382cf590fc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-130807" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-8){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}49 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77673d0c-9f03-5915-b2ab-4a5d6cb2d94d" :knowledge-point/title "Inverting a Frobenius Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b25f38b-636f-5de0-9797-eccc6742c19c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311023} {:answer/id #uuid "84719427-b4e8-59d4-b969-c3e635109c07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6} & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311024} {:answer/id #uuid "1335d27f-d44b-58a8-b586-dbffda46877b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{6} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311025} {:answer/id #uuid "8a80d01f-3d07-50bc-84c7-70282935a7e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311026} {:answer/id #uuid "2f4e4587-c1cc-5bcb-b0a6-b223c3c8e9e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{1}{6} & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a80d01f-3d07-50bc-84c7-70282935a7e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311026} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c09309c-f061-569e-8c77-bf6cc59031c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7852050f-67a6-578c-976b-548dc012848b" :question/math-academy-id "q-135395" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we're given a Frobenius matrix:\n\n- all entries on the main diagonal are ones\n\n- the matrix has only one column (the $1$st column) where some entries below the main diagonal are not zeros\n\n- all the other entries outside the main diagonal are zeros\n\nTo find the inverse of a Frobenius matrix, we simply take the non-zero entries outside the main diagonal with opposite signs. \n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de97ba5b-7e05-5f3d-b11d-bf8e10c3bc54" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Generalized Eigenvector of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4836cd99-8949-51d8-b090-f062ac317564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369065} {:answer/id #uuid "c36ebd00-683a-5e3c-9a28-ade34577392c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369066} {:answer/id #uuid "a17252a6-31dc-5d50-a563-9e0f116a754e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369067} {:answer/id #uuid "75299007-da8f-58fd-a7f1-9b1339182643" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369068} {:answer/id #uuid "ff4a5a41-6436-54f6-99ed-8854f0ba75d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a17252a6-31dc-5d50-a563-9e0f116a754e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369067} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccc5fe84-e0eb-5c89-8ed3-a3003bceca78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b76a4e9-0550-5e9b-86c9-b6dc6aa2f3d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-139448" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-5I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,(A-5I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-5I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f32c1ca-5ea4-5bc6-9eef-a9a7617398aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369072} {:answer/id #uuid "cd71f260-59ae-5c57-be24-c4d8d32e148b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369073} {:answer/id #uuid "d12ea080-cff9-5044-ac40-ef37c623517c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369074} {:answer/id #uuid "fe147d5c-ed6c-55f7-8a2f-0f6524262929" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369075} {:answer/id #uuid "c7dc72ae-b933-5509-95b6-26b3ca2355ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7dc72ae-b933-5509-95b6-26b3ca2355ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369076} :answer-field/id #uuid "266295c1-cb88-5e0b-a120-392a3af6ca3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "499f8d57-6ade-5414-a50c-fa707a48dd35" :question/math-academy-id "q-140842" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A+8I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $\\text{rank}(A+8I)=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+8I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that if $M=A+8I$ is singular, then $(A+8I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+8I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+8I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2480cdbb-3eea-5c96-bb8f-edcdbea7e886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369079} {:answer/id #uuid "0f6578c2-3e2a-59ca-9395-cf95e3481042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{50}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369080} {:answer/id #uuid "2e97dc51-6646-5265-ace0-e86a9d3b1bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369081} {:answer/id #uuid "c30e77af-1243-526a-9dd2-9fac58e17d39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369082} {:answer/id #uuid "6adb2792-0256-5852-a7e2-ec880e34fbd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{62}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e97dc51-6646-5265-ace0-e86a9d3b1bb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369081} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac69e96d-fec1-590b-97ef-264b252bbc4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4fc2267c-cf00-5edb-8fcf-dca3892e059b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143876" :question/problem "Three baskets, $A,B,$ and $C,$ contain pears. It is known that $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the pears from basket $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ of those from basket $B$ are ripe. A basket is selected at random, and a pear is chosen at random from the basket. If the probability that the selected pear is ripe is $\\frac{1}{4},$  what proportion of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the pear is ripe, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a ripe pear, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from baskets $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{5},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from one of the baskets is $\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{15}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{180} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{23}{60}$ of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f6f3aab-e069-585c-a5dd-dcba5897f9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.872" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369086} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0fd95d-e9c3-5cb1-99ca-c48ae9d97099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.664" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369087} {:answer/id #uuid "92f65170-b156-5f2e-8b59-42e60774b300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.923" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369088} {:answer/id #uuid "e44e619d-6352-5d7a-b131-f45b826bfe80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.885" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369089} {:answer/id #uuid "73a703bd-afbc-5e88-956f-88eb56fc44ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.943" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac0fd95d-e9c3-5cb1-99ca-c48ae9d97099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.664" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369087} :answer-field/id #uuid "2654228b-08dd-5bec-83b0-6f72a011e1be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a8c2e20f-0f73-51af-a685-5cac68a35ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-148669" :question/problem "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ gets $36%$ of the copies.\n\n- Distributor $2$ gets $23%$ of the copies, and $92%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ gets $41%$ of the copies, and $95%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $3%$ of all copies have some printing defect, $70%$ of defective copies are sold, and $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is given to distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.36 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.23 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.92 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.41 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.36+0.23+0.41=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold, which is represented as $P(S|{D}_{1}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.03)}{P(S|{D}_{1})(0.36)+(0.92)(0.23)+(0.95)(0.41)} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{D}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.025(0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011) & =0.021 \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.025} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011 \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.36}(\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011) \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & ≈0.664\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd2024a9-31c2-5ab4-8f1b-5b3d44d87152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369093} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f485c8-8cd5-5a48-ab7e-35be33d34082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369094} {:answer/id #uuid "da0f71ac-ac79-53a5-8242-505aad22a02a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369095} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c05471-f12d-55e7-9901-267fe90e8ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369096} {:answer/id #uuid "254097d6-f674-5cee-b836-1f0fb8e188ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5c05471-f12d-55e7-9901-267fe90e8ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369096} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cf89217-10fb-502b-bb7f-a7ab0f96783e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7644e0cb-fe7e-5af8-a7f6-5b53f6f2ec86" :question/math-academy-id "q-161114" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/5b5b5e82eb8dca9ae71c08b306ac4618500c7c3af4aa0858297d5585e055007f.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $4$ and $6,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $4$ and $6$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36,$ we obtain $r=6.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{6}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{6}\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[r]}_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (6-4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c571bc2-35c7-570d-a288-b1a921ab5fc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360499} {:answer/id #uuid "73de1ff4-ee8d-51cf-91a8-0c042cc2db9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360500} {:answer/id #uuid "c7866a8d-9be9-5327-b333-d494ad8459d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360501} {:answer/id #uuid "880393eb-753a-546c-9dc4-dfe6e16927c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360502} {:answer/id #uuid "d81299ca-c205-557f-92d3-4842f71314a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7866a8d-9be9-5327-b333-d494ad8459d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360501} :answer-field/id #uuid "5012f3a7-3b63-5e7e-b09a-8167a80cfe4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49e5c10d-6a90-5598-9eca-de58bdca7cbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-162403" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{2t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{2t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}|}{{((2{e}^{2t})^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{e}^{t}}{{(4{e}^{4t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{6{e}^{(0)}}{{(4{e}^{4(0)}+{e}^{-2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8af1a7f0-21ea-599e-8ae4-8c70b697fdc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294582} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd70fd7-4169-593d-b17d-6524c8aa73d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294583} {:answer/id #uuid "99bea051-55e3-5f06-9ebe-be38839b0a5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294584} {:answer/id #uuid "97e80f02-5fc0-5dbe-8691-00ee2ab26405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294585} {:answer/id #uuid "cc59692d-8e56-544e-8518-ed211520a029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97e80f02-5fc0-5dbe-8691-00ee2ab26405" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294585} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bfa03b0-59e8-5335-aa32-e5c6bb8083d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0d1d1a5-d9cb-5570-acc7-da72657b9a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163548" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "037ff08b-3246-5eed-870b-b4951f319c96" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Hessian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4223c84a-633e-5d34-ae57-21c4f7de5091" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364971} {:answer/id #uuid "02349ac6-0b22-5fb1-8e57-19399fecf3f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364972} {:answer/id #uuid "e27acc18-d276-599f-b725-1840f8eb24ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364973} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f1b36e-b82e-52a3-99d4-bd6f57fbe987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364974} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0c6bec-1135-5071-931f-2dc14d4eb0b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e27acc18-d276-599f-b725-1840f8eb24ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364973} :answer-field/id #uuid "6aae77dd-804e-5791-9b66-5a8e698e4c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364970}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c30dd99-4223-5fc5-b0a9-d42d9d800191" :question/math-academy-id "q-163642" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det(\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}-6xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=4x-6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4x-6y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4y & 4x-6y \\\\ 4x-6y & -6x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-28.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a11a371-8d2e-5a1d-a12d-9530631a461b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369100} {:answer/id #uuid "6f64d774-b919-5dc1-b559-39561ffea51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369101} {:answer/id #uuid "d74da73c-8972-5c48-8009-284af2146857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369102} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4206cd-1af1-553b-8b9c-3c3c6a36b438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369103} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c01546-7de0-52d2-beb7-ff217799abec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f64d774-b919-5dc1-b559-39561ffea51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369101} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c3727fc-202d-57f2-ab46-0fca87ebaebe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b9a0044-c09a-5774-bb14-17d82777ff05" :question/math-academy-id "q-164830" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & ≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ -(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "625252f5-70b7-5519-8288-50aa0773fcdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365719} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef8257a-155e-5b88-a037-e88562d62ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365720} {:answer/id #uuid "61974dae-c296-55f0-922e-a3ba72c070d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365721} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4abef3-6f17-5e7c-b416-d7da97b7c2eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365722} {:answer/id #uuid "367ee035-9424-5179-adb4-74c4e4ded90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61974dae-c296-55f0-922e-a3ba72c070d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365721} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b91bd78-5134-55ef-bafc-4c63534b5298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64cc5ba2-54af-56a0-8677-64acabcf0828" :question/math-academy-id "q-164831" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =5, & \\,{f}^{′}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =5, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2) & ≈5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5+6+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =11+\\frac{1}{2}(44) \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f19dadc-9a47-59fe-8d57-14ca9a0f095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320372} {:answer/id #uuid "59ca6c49-6b6b-5efa-9d96-1f6004f090cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{-1}{1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320373} {:answer/id #uuid "f814935a-3005-55a2-aacc-7b2a0929f3ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{x+C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320374} {:answer/id #uuid "58d16a85-9e04-5f05-b7d5-8cf620b3a60b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{-1+Cx}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320375} {:answer/id #uuid "197b642a-007d-568a-ab5d-e44698445089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{-1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f19dadc-9a47-59fe-8d57-14ca9a0f095a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320372} :answer-field/id #uuid "98047d80-b5b5-5e6e-82bb-eb5ebd0f6a0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23f7cb90-301b-5baf-815d-3ddcf267e164" :question/math-academy-id "q-17552" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}={y}^{2}-y\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution* $z={y}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{z} \\\\ -\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-1+z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-1,\\,Q(x)=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-x}$ yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}-z) & ={e}^{-x}\\cdot (-1) \\\\ {e}^{-x}\\frac{dz}{dx}-{e}^{-x}z & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-x}) & =-{e}^{-x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-x})\\,dx\\, & =-\\int {e}^{-x}\\,dx \\\\ z{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-x}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =1+C{e}^{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{x}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43d32bc2-4073-5381-b98a-567bc2a48223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325550} {:answer/id #uuid "67d4f15c-1970-5c8f-9633-9d0d2238c0a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325551} {:answer/id #uuid "fc69be5c-c48c-5ba0-97b7-1eb82db7b44c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325552} {:answer/id #uuid "f9ef9151-5076-5e05-b513-372963712e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325553} {:answer/id #uuid "55f9ef50-b404-5db7-8f65-687d158f834f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55f9ef50-b404-5db7-8f65-687d158f834f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325554} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c173abd-1245-5684-a5f3-018c5be6619b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2025eb0b-c186-520b-9049-e64b856c84a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335251" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (-1,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (1,2)$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+tx)}{1+{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (-1,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since $1+tx>0$). Computing the  partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(1+tx)(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (-1,1).$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (1,2)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,2)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (1,2).$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{3}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "556df5ed-b540-58fd-b44a-adb7ddad3eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369107} {:answer/id #uuid "4074a504-6e17-5bd5-80dc-58b7e403bf1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369108} {:answer/id #uuid "a74401ea-309f-5294-b052-678f1a681ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369109} {:answer/id #uuid "f558a2c9-9cf1-5bef-90d3-9a12cde26dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369110} {:answer/id #uuid "ef756984-575a-5523-8188-1244943c4e9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4074a504-6e17-5bd5-80dc-58b7e403bf1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369108} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdb18c16-9cae-5b03-a649-2f26b297d661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "765540e0-6bf9-5c09-a934-b0d2b992dba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-335282" :question/problem "The volume $X(t)$ (in $\\text{L}$) of water in a container as it fills toward its maximum capacity can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial volume is $1.5\\,\\text{L},$ how long does it take for the container to reach a volume of $2\\,\\text{L}?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the container's volume is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dX}{3-X} & =0.4\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dX}{3-X} & =\\int 0.4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-X| & =0.4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $3-X>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $X(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-X & ={e}^{-0.4t-C} \\\\ 3-X & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ 3-X & =K{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ X & =3-K{e}^{-0.4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial volume was $1.5\\,\\text{L}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $X(0)=1.5,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5 & =3-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 1.5 & =3-K \\\\ K & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nX(t)=3-1.5{e}^{-0.4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the volume is $2\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X({t}_{1}) & =2 \\\\ 3-1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ 1 & =1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -0.4{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =1.013… \\\\  & ≈1\\,\\text{minute},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "11810d4b-610c-5dac-8fe7-abf9391c2aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Objects"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ece31cc5-4493-571f-be4f-43a27342195e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ece31cc5-4493-571f-be4f-43a27342195e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294661} :answer-field/id #uuid "cfb1e79f-7789-5ee9-8707-8979eaf696ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294660} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c18ce72-f6b8-5ce2-bf10-d7edc42cee9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c18ce72-f6b8-5ce2-bf10-d7edc42cee9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294663} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c91fc5c-380e-5e71-92bf-b258b18e1dfa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "386b24bd-3eff-5335-834a-d78cada3240c" :question/math-academy-id "q-336300" :question/problem "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(3.6\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(2.8\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(4.5\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (2.8\\times {10}^{6})}{3.6\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 4.5\\cdot 2.8}{3.6}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{6}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =23.345\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{23.345\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{23.345}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.8316…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.8\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Shortest Distance Between an Osculating Plane and the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90dafe4a-c60d-5a22-af27-196cd1b848ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117737} {:answer/id #uuid "ed925136-3220-53c2-a267-e958a9402525" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117738} {:answer/id #uuid "2208379e-c8da-5db5-85d1-059ea5abbb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4π}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117739} {:answer/id #uuid "3248ef06-0e12-5038-a0e6-b5dffde6689c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117740} {:answer/id #uuid "5de8357e-540b-5140-a3e5-e922a03973a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2208379e-c8da-5db5-85d1-059ea5abbb59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4π}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117739} :answer-field/id #uuid "f732a7f2-a1d9-599e-865c-b4a9cb818912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5eba7f51-df52-5732-a9c9-7fd19c4deaa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-33732" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,4t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,4,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,4,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,4(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=⟨-1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\times ⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,j-4\\,k) \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨-1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩| \\\\  & =|\\,0+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}+0\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55abc561-2d1a-5da7-80f0-f52ea8c1bc3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Trajectories of a One-Parameter Family"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6e8a51f-03c5-59c0-b027-d554459af78c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=Ky" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294669} {:answer/id #uuid "712138d7-e620-5be5-b342-8a3843ff1257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294670} {:answer/id #uuid "88282ac6-c4c6-50a3-a829-eed1fc5efb79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Kx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294671} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f73c9d-7bbc-5af3-8bdc-37f401b89654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy=K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294672} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6daef7-cda8-562b-b48f-730cd7e06337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a6daef7-cda8-562b-b48f-730cd7e06337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294673} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bca0655-c64d-5fdc-9b72-2e0dc772b824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "494759ac-e110-5f70-93f7-3b6af7555034" :question/math-academy-id "q-337570" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ y+x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/98159dfb60fd280468ad8165475b5ba58e41658174a932b4c9555ef1153a577c.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a524bc-dbe0-527a-9025-3d60ee66662c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369114} {:answer/id #uuid "4f701a29-414c-5242-af93-500e7082a757" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369115} {:answer/id #uuid "2263fea8-99ab-5528-9114-c1a5f4d13e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369116} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbfd963-2906-5722-b152-e3c6726d4626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369117} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3d0790-793b-5370-982b-173ea365929b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2263fea8-99ab-5528-9114-c1a5f4d13e64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369116} :answer-field/id #uuid "210210bc-cd82-5454-b1dc-84e092e994aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6d64847-1769-5602-97fa-762ad6861828" :question/math-academy-id "q-338142" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{cos}^{2}⁡2x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}({cos}^{2}⁡2x+{sin}^{2}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2\\int dx \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation Whose Characteristic Equation Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d95791-2cc7-5a8b-962f-73c236a5c448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369121} {:answer/id #uuid "de4cbfee-dc26-5f53-9de8-a54d1fed0a56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369122} {:answer/id #uuid "bc64cc80-c370-594d-8ddb-00cdb5c8bcb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369123} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc4f3e-ef1a-51f8-837a-6b1ed0325ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369124} {:answer/id #uuid "c11e43fe-ba7a-53c0-90d7-a59aa3b96b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc4f3e-ef1a-51f8-837a-6b1ed0325ebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369124} :answer-field/id #uuid "01fdd2a3-162b-5f4d-bd71-bfbd4cb30ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb7b8734-1edc-5c36-87f9-ef5befb2dd32" :question/math-academy-id "q-338767" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+5y=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{{x{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "734fe467-57d6-5b3f-a61e-1e2e4624b5fc" :knowledge-point/title "Using Abel's Identity to Conclude Independence/Dependence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6e05566-95b8-564c-9cfc-913d0f6fa03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294721} {:answer/id #uuid "df881ed4-b6ec-5b5a-89cf-5b98f3e117ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{Q(x)}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294722} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b052f0-e079-5c09-bd11-7e534e01b874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{Q(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294723} {:answer/id #uuid "e91d7c62-251e-57d9-8ed3-113e72949674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e91d7c62-251e-57d9-8ed3-113e72949674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294724} :answer-field/id #uuid "31d63072-94ae-5b91-9e6e-8feb402b4ae9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294720} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbdb9a95-ba14-5e69-9187-8655c2d40495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294726} {:answer/id #uuid "b303f4f7-2d61-5f92-b2e2-3604679f8f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294727} {:answer/id #uuid "a30010e8-ab17-5838-a398-d794b51a0505" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294728} {:answer/id #uuid "7b8631c6-9ee6-53b3-ac9b-dc359c495d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b8631c6-9ee6-53b3-ac9b-dc359c495d85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294729} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae983504-8e5c-5bc8-839c-eb1f0e78a6d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294725} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d417faed-38a1-5dca-a375-e4508e2e18e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294731} {:answer/id #uuid "cd9c5e01-1b30-5a5b-bde4-df7c8649e36f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294732} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfdb94a-0599-5e19-8202-e3d36c030c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d417faed-38a1-5dca-a375-e4508e2e18e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294731} :answer-field/id #uuid "69f067df-2c92-5450-9de2-4a884870ae7f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "736a842a-a005-5f7c-a75f-80d3e3e641b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343302" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=2.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=2,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(0) & =C{e}^{-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)} \\\\ 2 & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)\\ne 0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369126 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0dae42c-61ff-5b67-bdf3-3cef1606a783" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369128} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa8fa5c-f847-59f7-bdde-4368b38f88f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369129} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0dbd04-0acd-5318-b5ba-53d0564d4a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369130} {:answer/id #uuid "da78d2ec-409f-5514-8467-a0e722e40c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369131} {:answer/id #uuid "d52a12f7-7409-52db-9689-0c43c46a9fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da78d2ec-409f-5514-8467-a0e722e40c14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369131} :answer-field/id #uuid "77da9420-ee03-5ff5-a08e-1c47ca45eb35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db727116-3b09-56e5-8d24-96a8c59d2a82" :question/math-academy-id "q-343739" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f17327b-1293-5d02-9614-56cb5bc32a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369135} {:answer/id #uuid "1632a717-fa9b-5a41-b58e-6cbba7f3e9bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369136} {:answer/id #uuid "5be7ba38-0784-532d-823b-79721c845b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369137} {:answer/id #uuid "30dcb20d-e5bc-50aa-a16d-c0cb4a88e0e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369138} {:answer/id #uuid "dca16548-7f00-5f60-a5f4-041400b2a11a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f17327b-1293-5d02-9614-56cb5bc32a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369135} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7490e5a-e807-53a6-b133-4feed29aa08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "512495a2-8bfb-52b3-bf2e-01e8a787e6fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343761" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=-2.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f90c55-cb3e-5e62-bc60-f164febd0b2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294743} {:answer/id #uuid "feadf2cc-2fc5-5249-bd32-86e9aa39b5bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={cos}^{2}⁡(2x)+2yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294744} {:answer/id #uuid "ef66f3d1-c36f-5352-bab1-49341b797fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294745} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9e840b-18d7-5ddc-b240-a471593eba06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294746} {:answer/id #uuid "457428cc-98f3-5ea0-aca9-79cb1f0e72ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef66f3d1-c36f-5352-bab1-49341b797fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294745} :answer-field/id #uuid "60c5e6c4-7dc7-56e4-97c0-75ffef545d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f48dae94-b20d-5d85-9a71-53e264ee506a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36978" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,i+2yz\\,j+{y}^{2}\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (2yz)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={y}^{2}z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)={y}^{2}z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) & ={y}^{2} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{y}^{2}z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8725caed-481c-5fbe-a0c4-cffdcb308881" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vector Given With Respect to the Standard Basis Under a Linear Map"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa3cf698-10b5-52fd-90c8-078f90164003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369142} {:answer/id #uuid "42c3c456-cf9f-513c-9898-92864d99b1c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369143} {:answer/id #uuid "26a2b8f6-9638-5b35-99d0-eac09f89ccfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369144} {:answer/id #uuid "beb87bdf-8bde-5622-915a-0064157dbb99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369145} {:answer/id #uuid "49086f4c-a404-5e64-84cc-cc41a883c591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42c3c456-cf9f-513c-9898-92864d99b1c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369143} :answer-field/id #uuid "1accf33e-bbe4-520e-8bdd-0f6f5f73bcaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b03e0038-023a-5069-84d1-998d6d535a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-38540" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[x]_{B},$ the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B.$ So, let\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "616b7aa2-2c55-5a29-a908-545a4bad4376" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e78f7d83-8f03-50a7-98ca-15315cdaa1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369149} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf9317b-89ed-519e-93b9-193c5f3cd387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369150} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1b613e-f4b3-52f3-bf0c-27b40021a8c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369151} {:answer/id #uuid "83a6ed1d-84b6-5db6-8099-6e8d5d474f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369152} {:answer/id #uuid "6393cbad-9a82-5290-abe1-d2c63b65f26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bf9317b-89ed-519e-93b9-193c5f3cd387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369150} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5f0c6b-6959-594d-9567-93b7821e26a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c49a23bb-a7fc-57e9-921a-fc537e2e5220" :question/math-academy-id "q-42567" :question/problem "In a particular city, about $30%$ of mornings are cloudy and $40%$ of days are rainy. \nIt is known that $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings. What is the probability that a randomly selected cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6\\cdot 0.4}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37209e5f-a6e8-55d5-b76f-19f79db67566" :knowledge-point/title "Computing 4x4 Determinants Using Row and Column Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d41a26e-4c34-529a-9f07-9c3a331390a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294329} {:answer/id #uuid "b2419836-5865-5ec6-b639-b2dca741c0af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294330} {:answer/id #uuid "f57cf019-138f-5826-83eb-991f6aac57a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294331} {:answer/id #uuid "e885b534-d733-57eb-b0a9-e0d0f540779a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294332} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d18489-db79-5815-a081-d4edc5fd9420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6d18489-db79-5815-a081-d4edc5fd9420" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294333} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd0771f6-8065-5439-a3d4-c10a760e8167" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7b429e2-c38b-50a3-87f8-3dc384ee9f6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42890" :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{12}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" :question/worked-solution "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries below ${a}_{12}=2$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(4-3) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5481ebe6-7afc-5959-b9ff-3ac4af78b201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294336} {:answer/id #uuid "30ebfb58-3bc7-569d-95bf-f02e3b8ca8a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294337} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7679e4-a83a-5caf-a580-1ef94cc88f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294338} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c9dce6-e0bc-52df-a671-6796ea08e690" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294339} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4dd53b-c48e-5dad-815f-a941a41f4766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5481ebe6-7afc-5959-b9ff-3ac4af78b201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294336} :answer-field/id #uuid "506d02c0-5619-5a27-a023-c555e254176f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "549da073-b722-5fcb-9360-923ddd086ff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-43780" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[-1,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ and its subset $A={f\\in C[-1,1]\\,|\\,f(1)\\le 0}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under function addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of $C[-1,1]$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of $C[-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(x),g(x)\\in A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(1) & =f(1)+g(1)\\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)\\in A$ and $k=-1\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & \\le 0 \\\\ kf(1) & \\ge k\\cdot 0 \\\\ (kf) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $A$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36986a18-172c-5983-8f6f-3211b6e8f89d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Curvature of a Plane Curve Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9777d1a5-c0d1-5e45-a5c5-8804d9a99224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119032} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2e66a9-128e-571e-8b39-e64df411f919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119033} {:answer/id #uuid "1816e76f-b533-5aa6-a5b0-dee6d82320ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119034} {:answer/id #uuid "5c618108-f77b-578b-a3ec-390444c1ccfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119035} {:answer/id #uuid "784bf460-93ab-573f-92f2-1afac72cdaba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1816e76f-b533-5aa6-a5b0-dee6d82320ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119034} :answer-field/id #uuid "c318bce1-18de-5fa1-b45d-da4ab5a7464b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186119031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf912f7e-2809-5344-9478-be3125239509" :question/math-academy-id "q-64901" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot {e}^{t}|}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+(-{e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{2}{{({e}^{2(0)}+{e}^{-2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{2}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369154 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06fce26b-7020-5f61-9971-36933a80e252" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369156} {:answer/id #uuid "2814d360-7194-570f-9f65-58f8bb9ca8cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369157} {:answer/id #uuid "c8aa9617-31a4-53fc-8878-4b3f76800e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369158} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddafe21-3567-526b-b2ae-185a6f8c0bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369159} {:answer/id #uuid "7424d9fe-e76a-5a0e-b498-ea5cfa88774b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7424d9fe-e76a-5a0e-b498-ea5cfa88774b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369160} :answer-field/id #uuid "02a4ddfa-5f0b-5d3d-9cb6-b34db3ad5044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b171da41-b950-5445-bd40-70968beeadd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-65145" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $0$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da666821-a92c-58e8-ae5c-e9ca2f31d8c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2/3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369163} {:answer/id #uuid "53b1eaf4-603b-5176-9a44-cc72731259da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2/3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/2}=1." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369164} {:answer/id #uuid "77809515-85bf-5517-861f-53ef115df517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3/2}=1." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369165} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0fe807-be82-513c-af75-0c010b3822a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369166} {:answer/id #uuid "f20531fc-cf32-5341-9c48-ff9deeae35c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{1/2}=1." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c0fe807-be82-513c-af75-0c010b3822a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369166} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd3832a9-d2dd-5ba0-87a9-c9aee6a7039e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33ec3330-aeaf-5e9b-a359-02e080019320" :question/math-academy-id "q-73257" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1$ and the plane $z-y=3$ onto the $xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y=3\\,⟹\\,z=y+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{3{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+2y+3}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+2y+3}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{({y}^{2}+2y+1)+2}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}+2}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536de9bd-b925-5fde-9a18-7659e6ac81d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333813} {:answer/id #uuid "0e698b23-e674-562b-9725-672ace33dc69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333814} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4df8ba-9dfc-5923-84a3-872dca158f01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333815} {:answer/id #uuid "071f479b-04d2-5caa-b345-9f4513703d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333816} {:answer/id #uuid "3cedb535-b6d1-504a-9105-51c5e70ffc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cedb535-b6d1-504a-9105-51c5e70ffc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333817} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d78575b-a3e0-576b-b5ff-36e8ed92f2f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "538bab68-7058-5f4f-b133-c515348f8f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-74964" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $2$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3d1a48d-b303-5108-bf3d-41cf2b142017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369170} {:answer/id #uuid "3e591d12-74c2-5577-b196-484985280fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369171} {:answer/id #uuid "320ddc7c-7c7d-541e-9ebe-9ac46df72c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369172} {:answer/id #uuid "2f8f7c53-d3cc-5bfe-9343-72ec3b72a943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369173} {:answer/id #uuid "542a575b-c995-5c99-8b0f-3f774e748289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d1a48d-b303-5108-bf3d-41cf2b142017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369170} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d173091-98b1-5f9e-ba14-223fe159f379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cfde08b6-0b33-56dc-ab2e-672049a30b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79811" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0.5,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0.5,1) & =2\\cdot 0.5\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0.5+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-0.5=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+1\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368255 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649" :knowledge-point/title "Using Differentials to Approximate the Change in a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da38700e-6cef-5a76-b977-fb143e5f3ce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368257} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc2660c-89f6-5af0-9c43-45260bd1415c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368258} {:answer/id #uuid "8c38c489-c392-56d7-b827-3d7519be8373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368259} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce2bbef-c135-5197-8d10-2ee0d2def449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368260} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0fef72-835c-53ea-9e68-78c0ca90383f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab0fef72-835c-53ea-9e68-78c0ca90383f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368261} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b9b42f-9987-54bc-bd68-413c1c8d2bab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dfed89f-88e1-5c5a-a0c4-8c6a48d5109c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79812" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0,e)$ to $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0,e),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0,e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{0}{e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0+Δx,e+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0,e)$ to $(1,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-0=1, \\\\ Δy & =2-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+0\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (2-e) \\\\  & ≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f4bd05-1f30-576c-a538-a0ac4d533b7a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Plane of a Surface Given Implicitly Using the Gradient Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a46a3bc-9089-599a-a63f-9fc68a7eeeb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369177} {:answer/id #uuid "38718a47-8236-522f-8bab-75d4a7aed904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369178} {:answer/id #uuid "e312aa09-d16e-5084-a55f-f0d28e042abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369179} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8d3569-d110-5d71-8155-d4acf556257c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369180} {:answer/id #uuid "1e895897-5109-50c8-9869-355a5ce0aea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38718a47-8236-522f-8bab-75d4a7aed904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369178} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b31f61a-2eb7-5caa-8974-71cfeeb1b2f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "229858c7-5c8f-5030-8583-21587b3271c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80778" :question/problem "Consider the surface ${2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}=2.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,-1,1),$ what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=2.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})k \\\\  & =({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)i+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)j+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(y\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }i+x\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }j+3z\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }k) \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2((-1)\\cdot \\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }i+1\\cdot \\,\\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }j+3(1)\\cdot {2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-{2}^{1}\\text{ }i+{2}^{1}\\text{ }j+3\\cdot {2}^{1}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-i+j+3\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fffe5aa-ee8a-5b26-8ade-c9c24234c182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367849} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8805f9-1c6e-521c-a98a-cc0f79fe6a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367850} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6a9dad-8386-5818-983d-4906ea5a545f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367851} {:answer/id #uuid "b212765d-6088-51dd-bcb2-b84794cb59e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367852} {:answer/id #uuid "b36e4cb5-c904-56fb-a24e-dd76af5a2e67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fffe5aa-ee8a-5b26-8ade-c9c24234c182" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367849} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d6d9648-4bd5-5a09-8c96-d66b096cb920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "defa1cb1-365b-5e5f-ab67-ca3d065551d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-81190" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ be linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\nGiven that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ find the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-3\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -2 & 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-1-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(1-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-3\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(1+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector to ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(5)(5)}{(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db422412-128c-551d-bfe7-009fa0d0a988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz+xz+xy+yz+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369184} {:answer/id #uuid "37c2d898-6a51-5075-9c1a-67842a6be680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369185} {:answer/id #uuid "28f0ba0d-36db-5319-9d3d-a60c9cdd8b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369186} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd4134d-765e-586f-851e-78f013e6d97a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369187} {:answer/id #uuid "51a8a8d3-3021-5096-952b-ca349320a568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz+x+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28f0ba0d-36db-5319-9d3d-a60c9cdd8b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369186} :answer-field/id #uuid "333026f2-f23d-583e-938d-a19470e048a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7429fa1e-a8b3-5b4c-87be-75aad7649b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81740" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(y+1)\\,i+(x+z+1)\\,j+(y+1)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xy+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x+z+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =z+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (z+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =yz+y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=yz+y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+x+yz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =1 \\\\ h(z) & =z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c" :knowledge-point/title "Swapping the Order of Integration on a Non-Partitioned Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6789d9af-6700-51dc-b2d2-5bbe7ed0b42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{y+2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369191} {:answer/id #uuid "e97ce313-8c83-56a3-8144-1a2d40385944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y+2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369192} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa40dda-3ced-5113-98cd-a86d6a837bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y+2}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369193} {:answer/id #uuid "07d44a2c-c9ca-59f6-b2b3-9b6d27a7ff0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{y+2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369194} {:answer/id #uuid "46cb55d5-8a50-5539-a5ab-94381bd4a411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y+2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fa40dda-3ced-5113-98cd-a86d6a837bcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y+2}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369193} :answer-field/id #uuid "c87e4d0f-6e2a-5ad3-977f-9b55fe24dd26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df7a9928-b595-5d80-8b20-2abd529873c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-82338" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{x-2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/75f9c68145ed74a2c3530012061eb65cd93bae38d82fa0d34e5dc042f8073841.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le x-2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x-2\\,⟹\\,x=y+2\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y-2\\le x\\le 4},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/575e7e482f88635f6267405f66857a5488dac79833fce16d3d998df19e24d119.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{x-2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y+2}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f4bd05-1f30-576c-a538-a0ac4d533b7a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Plane of a Surface Given Implicitly Using the Gradient Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b804cd3b-2565-5b06-9eb6-ef89ff621a63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369198} {:answer/id #uuid "7713ced6-5f50-561f-b97b-b1226413d7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369199} {:answer/id #uuid "824a6509-df8e-5766-b650-e72371d6b7d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369200} {:answer/id #uuid "1607a6b1-fbb7-555b-9281-d7382dc69494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369201} {:answer/id #uuid "d047b765-74ce-5fad-ae09-fcf4764f9d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d047b765-74ce-5fad-ae09-fcf4764f9d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369202} :answer-field/id #uuid "c05fc4c1-d7d4-542c-ad70-48a1a0bcff8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d36da64c-0744-5c6d-adbb-e4cbe16d0433" :question/math-academy-id "q-86471" :question/problem "Consider the surface ${x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3}=4.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(2,-1,0),$ what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})k \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{4})i+(4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+{z}^{4}{e}^{yz})j+({z}^{2}{e}^{yz}(yz+3))k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,0) & =2(2)(-1)^{4}\\text{ }i+[4(2)^{2}(-1)^{3}+{0}^{4}{e}^{(-1)(0)}]\\text{ }j+[{0}^{2}{e}^{(-1)(0)}((-1)(0)+3)]\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\text{ }i-16\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-16,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨4,\\,-16,\\,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-16}{4}=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" :knowledge-point/title "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ef1b710-a3f0-5447-a9e9-c53014f598d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-17 & 30 \\\\ -9 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306868} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc45458-19b1-5438-9f0f-4ef79ff85392" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306869} {:answer/id #uuid "232a5974-ded3-5a6c-a231-23c4eef5215b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 19 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306870} {:answer/id #uuid "8d839acd-7543-59a8-a16a-041acb3aaf6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306871} {:answer/id #uuid "dbd04ec0-b98b-5749-ae26-f1005f26dfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d839acd-7543-59a8-a16a-041acb3aaf6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306871} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac6deca-d8f8-517a-a24a-93496cb68beb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f76ba1b-7ea5-5d91-91ae-a14e891b8be6" :question/math-academy-id "q-86676" :question/problem "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $G$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $G,$ then $G$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $G=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $G,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nG={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a485039c-7553-52e4-9f8d-b01f2b27c8ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369205} {:answer/id #uuid "5df26e7f-a36a-52de-9d2f-769e31017ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369206} {:answer/id #uuid "498192d5-ad48-5291-aab2-3b51e4c58401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369207} {:answer/id #uuid "84e535f8-5cf3-50df-ae2d-852b3ca8abe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369208} {:answer/id #uuid "86d403b6-3b13-5ebd-b33c-5fbba101e04c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86d403b6-3b13-5ebd-b33c-5fbba101e04c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369209} :answer-field/id #uuid "8798d2d1-2c90-5c9f-8a4f-a8a92aba57df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9246e15-4875-5686-8139-62f5acf7f760" :question/math-academy-id "q-94377" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=32t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+16t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+8t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a linear function, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t, \\\\ y(t) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7fd9a1-349b-5b8e-8f71-4b17b56d5d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351278} {:answer/id #uuid "b2cb348c-0172-5197-961c-11cf29367f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351279} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d59f86-dffc-588b-9b43-ee67ea32cf4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351280} {:answer/id #uuid "65dac41c-c0d7-5355-95dd-06cda0199205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351281} {:answer/id #uuid "666c985b-a1f1-5bf2-a63c-01e100bf3098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2cb348c-0172-5197-961c-11cf29367f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351279} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b1cae30-f8b9-5ce6-ad58-43e95762085d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e83ef381-2688-5e65-8917-dab31a31675d" :question/math-academy-id "q-94383" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion with damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=10{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}$\n- $y=R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+10{e}^{t}$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+41y=0.$ Writing down and solving the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +41 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16)-16+41 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +4 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-4\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(t)$ can be expressed in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{c}(t) & ={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ), \\\\ {y}_{c}(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ The particular solution is of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha {e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{5}.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={e}^{-4t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}, \\\\ y(t) & =R{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly I matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1979b91d-2ac0-5d35-b4cc-1c9c9505b503" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Inverse Tanh Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08412b52-4cfb-5412-8537-58ef35295219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294844} {:answer/id #uuid "1be660e5-4491-5cc2-a883-603f1c398d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294845} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0eb8f8-92ae-5019-9d5c-faa2978588e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{1-{e}^{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294846} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9c5272-40a5-58cd-912a-33a4cd05f646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294847} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f14128-a178-5bb3-afd9-439f540096b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08412b52-4cfb-5412-8537-58ef35295219" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294844} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d1a91e0-b7d1-5713-8e41-61b2622139c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bc13545-d6a0-5612-9a84-04aec6ad0b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95505" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{e}^{x},$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{artanh}⁡{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{({e}^{x})}^{2}}\\cdot {({e}^{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-{e}^{2x}}\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186327981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "273bab01-0930-5580-b073-8bafe7505887" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Square of the Hyperbolic Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "182d3b2b-1ff8-5162-9803-5cdc14fde36a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327983} {:answer/id #uuid "9ae7dbf8-9d35-59f4-8fb4-f5967daa274a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327984} {:answer/id #uuid "ceaf322b-af0a-5832-9ab1-5005664b314b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327985} {:answer/id #uuid "ac80e5c1-9497-5654-9017-aebc7b0be494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327986} {:answer/id #uuid "6adc60b9-17a8-5f75-a61a-a023629cf8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{coth}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae7dbf8-9d35-59f4-8fb4-f5967daa274a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327984} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d77f60d-e9ec-5547-bf6b-a7c070b2c1dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59346a59-f274-5f87-97f7-8c14ba6c65f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-96055" :question/problem "$\\int (2{csch}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t & \\,⇒\\,\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{coth}⁡t)=-{csch}^{2}⁡t & \\,⇒\\,\\int {csch}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{csch}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\int {csch}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+\\int \\operatorname{sinh}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡t)+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡t+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "389401a5-6afb-562c-9b74-51cde6742754" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369212} {:answer/id #uuid "794915b4-c2f3-5f27-8bf0-5af14901813f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369213} {:answer/id #uuid "068c9e7e-8f1e-5353-b607-c6b3e7cbdd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369214} {:answer/id #uuid "5373e557-c82a-5af5-843e-9b028b00e209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369215} {:answer/id #uuid "58a11bc8-a782-5f92-8920-c066e8821e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58a11bc8-a782-5f92-8920-c066e8821e8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369216} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f70b5f7-3b71-5355-aff9-d8df21fbbbec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7b6dd53-171d-534b-b5b5-b5fe81eabd1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-97078" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Line That Corresponds to a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbbf05b-8162-539c-8113-e859571ef9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/0b6a1907f045e737c1f136a47d941069efbfd23a389add17d1ae31cf336763b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369219} {:answer/id #uuid "96e98df2-7173-5032-9187-e75a8fcc7098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/a77fcb19143b7bdee347993a1da4fb6f73c5c1460c55ecab24ffde9afe7f6cf0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369220} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff6750e-5b03-5487-82ba-4c0f1e9b42e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/eb621888b04d5bd9b842624c278422995db44097adde61bbc769e8df0a5a47e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369221} {:answer/id #uuid "e74c42b7-d4a4-5ebd-ae89-0b7846a93317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369222} {:answer/id #uuid "9953192b-24ce-5a1e-8f27-04b379848220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/c5e335ffe5f2343671d1f9eca6cedc34ef34a9700837451e963cfcfe1cd153d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e74c42b7-d4a4-5ebd-ae89-0b7846a93317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369222} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd3ab181-a050-5268-94ed-94744cae622e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "519b7e3f-5e6b-5321-a66f-0a3fdc3bd943" :question/math-academy-id "q-98525" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+y-2?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png)"}]}
